  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHeight rises aloft and increasing warm swly surface flow \nstarting this evening (as a weak shortwave trough crosses north\nof Michigan) favors dry weather and rising temperature by Tue \nafternoon. Shorter period-of-record sites like New Philadelphia \nmay see highs that tie/break the daily record (20-30% \nprobability) while most others just experience highs that are \n10-15 deg above the daily averages.\n\nSubtle embedded shortwave troughs along the northern ridge \nperiphery will push a cold front swd into the region on Wed \nbefore the boundary stalls. Some uncertainty remains in how far\nsouth the boundary reaches before stalling, but at this time\nappears most likely near the I-70 corridor. This will favor a \ntrend toward seasonable temperature into Thu with rain showers \nmainly Wed afternoon/evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nEnsemble forecasts are favoring the development of a notable \nupper-level trough that will dive sewd through the Great Lakes \nregion starting early Fri morning and into Sat, with cluster \nanalysis favoring a modestly steep but progressive \ntrough/surface low system. The model mean pattern would lend to \nrain with potentially advisory-level wind gusts (and/or gusty \nwind-driven showers) Fri, waning precipitation with breezy but \nlow-impact wind gusts Sat, and a low probability (~10-15% \nchance) for lake-enhanced rain/snow showers Sat morning in \nnorthwest PA.\n\nCurrent guidance favors west-central PA to higher terrain \nlocations for wind gusts that reach Wind Advisory criterion\n(gusts of at least 46 mph) with non-zero probability of gusts\nreaching High Wind Warning threshold along ridge lines of ern \nTucker County. However, variance in frontal timing and depth of \npost-frontal mixing are the primary indicators to monitor in \nwhether wind risk become more/less likely in the days ahead.\n\nPrevious discussion of the first snowflakes of the year (and\neven accumulation) have taken a hit in the probabilistic space \ndue to some coalescence of modeled synoptic pattern of a more-\nprogressive system that both limits the degree of cold advection\nand duration of ideal lake-effect wind direction. Snow birds \nwill want to see ensemble trends toward a deeper, slower trough \n(mainly suggested by a handful of Canadian Ensemble Model \nmembers) in order to return higher probabilities of seeing the \nfirst flakes of the year.",
